## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of inter-process sharing via mmap, page cache semantics, and memory visibility without user-space locking.

## Key Concepts
- MAP_SHARED page mappings
- Page cache dirty bits and writeback
- Cache coherence and TLB effects
- Lightweight synchronization in shared memory (CAS, acquire/release)

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo: writer uses CAS to acquire lock, writes, then releases
uint32_t *lock = (uint32_t*)addr; // shared region
while (__sync_lock_test_and_set(lock, 1)) {}
// write 8 bytes to region after lock
memcpy((void*)(addr+8), data, 8);
__sync_lock_release(lock);

// Reader checks lock before reading
while (*lock) {} // wait for writer
memcpy(data, (void*)(addr+8), 8);
```

## Tradeoffs
Lock: Simple, ensures exclusive access but blocks readers during writes.
Seqlock: Readers can proceed concurrently but must retry if write occurs mid-read; better for read-heavy workloads.

## Why This Is Asked

Understanding safe concurrent data sharing is fundamental for OS-level IPC. This task tests practical use of atomics, memory ordering, and race testing.

## Key Concepts

- Ring buffer basics, head/tail invariants
- Atomic operations: CAS, fetch_add, acquire/release
- Memory ordering and visibility on multi-core CPUs
- Overflow handling and backpressure

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  _Atomic uint32_t head;
  _Atomic uint32_t tail;
  int slots[256];
} ring_t;

bool enqueue(ring_t *r, int val){
  uint32_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h >= 256) return false; // full
  r->slots[t & 255] = val;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(ring_t *r, int *out){
  uint32_t h = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
  uint32_t t = atomic_load_explicit(&r->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return false; // empty
  *out = r->slots[h & 255];
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

