## Why This Is Asked
Tests IPC basics, race-free data sharing, and practical use of CAS and memory barriers in a beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free synchronization
- Atomic operations and memory ordering
- Ring buffer wraparound
- Empty/full conditions and visibility

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { atomic_uint head, tail; Message buf[N]; } Channel;

bool send(Channel *c, Message m){
  unsigned long t = atomic_load_explicit(&c->tail, memory_order_relaxed);
  unsigned long w = (t + 1) % N;
  if (w == atomic_load_explicit(&c->head, memory_order_acquire)) return false; // full
  while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&c->tail, &t, w, memory_order_acq_rel, memory_order_relaxed));
  c->buf[t] = m;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool recv(Channel *c, Message *out){
  unsigned long h;
  do {
    h = atomic_load_explicit(&c->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    if (h == atomic_load_explicit(&c->tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false; // empty
  } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&c->head, &h, (h+1)%N, memory_order_acq_rel, memory_order_relaxed));
  *out = c->buf[h];
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add a timeout or backoff when the buffer is full/empty?
- How would you adapt this for multiple producers or consumers without locks?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and cross-platform memory barrier requirements in a production system context.

## Key Concepts
- MPMC ring buffer with per-slot sequence numbers
- ABA problem mitigation via publish/subscribe pattern
- Architecture-specific memory barrier requirements
- Contention handling and wraparound correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for enqueue/dequeue operations
bool enqueue(struct ring *r, void *data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Wait for slot to be available
    while (r->slots[slot].seq != pos) {
        // Backoff or handle contention
    }

    // Write payload
    r->slots[slot].data = data;

    // Publish with release barrier
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, pos + 1, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}

bool dequeue(struct ring *r, void **data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Check if slot is ready with acquire barrier
    size_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if (seq != pos + 1) return false;

    // Read payload
    *data = r->slots[slot].data;

    // Advance head
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->head, pos + 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return true;
}
```

## Memory Barriers by Architecture
- **x86_64**: `memory_order_release`/`memory_order_acquire` (no additional barriers needed)
- **ARM64**: `dmb ish` for release, `dmb ishld` for acquire operations

